60-    bottleneck at all, if what fills those hours is ordinary capacity that a trained person could
61-    take on Monday. The first business looks efficient and is fragile. The second looks exhausting
62:    and is robust. Nothing on a timesheet distinguishes them.</p>
63-
64-    <p>So the diagnostic is not <em>how long does this take?</em> but <em>what waits?</em> Four
65-    things can wait for you, and they fail differently:</p>
66-
67-    <ul>
68:      <li><strong>Recurring work</strong> — support, invoicing, the weekly deploy. Predictable, and
69-      the easiest to hand over, which is why owners start here and often stop here.</li>
70-      <li><strong>Exceptional decisions</strong> — the refund nobody has a rule for, the customer who
71-      wants a change to the contract, the quote outside the usual range. These are rare, which is why
--
