92-    <p>In March 2010, Patrick McKenzie published an account of running Bingo Card Creator — software
93:    that makes bingo cards for teachers — alongside a full-time job in Japan. The constraint was
94-    real and he documents it: <em>"I leave the office at 7:30 PM on a very good day, and have an
95:    hour and a half of commute both ways"</em>. Against that, the business's time cost was
96-    <em>"the peak was the week I launched (50 hours in 8 days)"</em>, a busy week that
97-    <em>"spiked up to as many as 20 hours, and the average over the period is (to my best estimate)
98:    about 5 hours"</em>. By the time he wrote it, the business had <em>"made it possible to quit my
99-    day job at the end of this month"</em>.</p>
100-
101-    <p>The method is the interesting part, and it is a method for workload. He divides everything
102:    into two ledgers: <em>"build time assets"</em> — things that save time later, whether code,
103:    documentation or marketing that keeps working — against time debt, where
104:    <em>"time debt is anything that you do which will commit you to doing unavoidable work in the
105-    future"</em>. Support email is converted from events into processes: a dozen messages are
106-    really <em>"six of Email A, 3 of Email B"</em> and three genuinely unusual ones, so A and B stop
107:    being email and become a FAQ entry, better copy, or a fix. <em>"Recurring tasks are a great thing
108-    to systemize and outsource."</em> And the scope of the whole thing is chosen to fit the
109-    constraint, because <em>"90% of the results of your business, and somewhere around 90% of the
110-    effort, are caused by non-coding activities"</em> — so the work that gets cut is chosen against
--
127-    customers throughout. <em>"Doing this on a live system is like performing kidney transplants on
128-    a playing mariachi band."</em></p>
129-
130:    <p>Set the two together. McKenzie's method is to build time assets — code that keeps working
131:    after the hours are spent — and his own definition of time debt is anything that commits you to
132-    unavoidable work later. Pinboard's core code was written in 2009 and 2010, the years that post
133-    was describing. By 2020 the same object was both: an asset that had run a business for a decade,
134-    and a piece of unavoidable work that could only be done by someone who knew it, done alone on a
135-    system that was serving customers throughout. Nothing in the intervening years' hours would have
136-    shown it, because it was never this week's problem. The record names no second operator and does
137:    not say whether another engineer could have done it with documentation and time —
138-    <strong>concentration that was never measured</strong>, not irreplaceability.</p>
