--
157-    customer who goes elsewhere, a certificate that expires, an opportunity with a date on it — is a
158-    bottleneck.</p>
159-
160:    <p>Rank what you find by <strong>tolerable delay</strong> rather than by how much time it takes.
161:    A four-hour task that cannot wait a day outranks a two-day task that can safely wait a month.
162:    This ordering is unintuitive and it is the point of the exercise: the things that consume the
163:    most time are usually the things you have already made routine, and the things that cannot wait
164-    are usually rare enough to feel harmless.</p>
165-
166-    <h3>The absence table sets the order, not the categories</h3>
167-
168:    <p><strong>The ranking rule governs: shortest tolerable delay first, from your own
169-    table.</strong> The four categories are for making sure you looked everywhere, and they will
170:    disagree with it — urgent support is recurring work with a one-hour tolerance, a deployment
171-    procedure is operational knowledge that stops the business at once, a safety or regulatory call
172:    outranks any password, and some access is recoverable in a week. Where they disagree, the table
173-    is right. With a blank table, look in this order:</p>
174-
175-    <ul>
176-      <li><strong>Access first</strong>, because losing it usually has the shortest fuse of anything
177-      on the list and some of it is fixable the same afternoon: accounts in the business's name, a
178-      second authorised person, a recovery procedure written down and tested rather than assumed.
179:      Little skill transfer is required, which is what makes it cheap. <strong>Relationships share the fuse and not the timescale</strong> — introducing a second person to your largest customer is months of ordinary contact, so it starts early and finishes late.</li>
180-      <li><strong>Exceptional decisions second</strong>, by writing the rule instead of making the
181-      call. A refund limit, a discount ceiling, a set of conditions under which the answer is yes
182-      without asking. Every decision you convert into a rule is one that stops waiting for you. A
--
186-      <li><strong>Operational knowledge third</strong>, and it is the slowest. It is the next
187-      chapter's subject, because knowledge that lives only in your head cannot be delegated by
188-      deciding to delegate it.</li>
189:      <li><strong>Recurring work last</strong> — where its tolerable delay allows, which for support
190-      often it does not. It is the most visible and the most annoying, and it is where owners start
191-      for exactly those reasons; but taking it off yourself while the other three are untouched buys
192:      you time in a business that still stops when you do.</li>
193-    </ul>
194-
195-    <h3>Automation can exchange visible labour for concealed dependence</h3>
196-
