chapters/18-writing-it-down.html-226-  <nav class="chapter-nav">
chapters/18-writing-it-down.html-227-    <a href="17-you-are-the-bottleneck.html"><span class="dir">Previous</span>17 · You Are the Bottleneck</a>
--
chapters/04-deciding-not-to.html-84-      revenue and five-figure custom development revenue.</p>
chapters/04-deciding-not-to.html-85-
chapters/04-deciding-not-to.html-86-      <p>She calls these <em>"what many consider to be validation
chapters/04-deciding-not-to.html-87-      milestones: paying customers, patents, and demonstrated clinical impact."</em></p>
chapters/04-deciding-not-to.html-88-
chapters/04-deciding-not-to.html-89-      <p>She drained her savings, and between 2023
chapters/04-deciding-not-to.html:90:      and early 2024 — five years in, the arithmetic still unrun — raised <strong>$265,000</strong> from family, friends and physicians. She pitched
chapters/04-deciding-not-to.html-91-      <em>"over 35 different VC firms, but they were not interested in funding a solo founder without
chapters/04-deciding-not-to.html-92-      any revenue."</em></p>
chapters/04-deciding-not-to.html-93-
chapters/04-deciding-not-to.html-94-      <p>Then she did the arithmetic that the seven years of milestones had never required her to do.
chapters/04-deciding-not-to.html-95-      The small practices she was targeting <em>"wanted to pay fewer than $100 per month."</em>
chapters/04-deciding-not-to.html-96-      Hosting and AI costs had risen to <em>"about $70 per doctor."</em> Reaching those practices
--
chapters/03-selling-it-before-it-exists.html-284-    thousand strangers made non-refundable payments, at $185, for an object that did not exist.</p>
chapters/03-selling-it-before-it-exists.html-285-
chapters/03-selling-it-before-it-exists.html-286-    <p>Now the second axis. In February 2015 there were no coolers. According to Matthew Hughes in
chapters/03-selling-it-before-it-exists.html-287-    <em>The Register</em>, the company had <em>"spruiked over 60,000 units costing $185 apiece"</em>
chapters/03-selling-it-before-it-exists.html-288-    and, two years after the campaign, <em>"asked backers for a further $97 in order for them to
chapters/03-selling-it-before-it-exists.html-289-    receive their product before the July 4 holidays of 2016."</em> A settlement with the Oregon
chapters/03-selling-it-before-it-exists.html:290:    Department of Justice followed. It shut down in December 2019 — five years past its delivery
chapters/03-selling-it-before-it-exists.html-291-    date — <em>"with 20,000 orders unfulfilled,"</em> owing those backers money that
chapters/03-selling-it-before-it-exists.html-292-    <em>"cannot be discharged in bankruptcy."</em></p>
chapters/03-selling-it-before-it-exists.html-293-
chapters/03-selling-it-before-it-exists.html-294-    <p>Why it collapsed is disputed and this chapter need not settle it; Grepper attributed the end
chapters/03-selling-it-before-it-exists.html-295-    to tariffs on Chinese imports, and there is no document here that would let anyone adjudicate
chapters/03-selling-it-before-it-exists.html-296-    that. The structural point survives whatever the cause. The pledge total was simultaneously the
--
chapters/16-enough.html-80-
chapters/16-enough.html-81-    <p>Pinboard is a bookmarking service that one person, Maciej Cegłowski, launched in July 2009 and
chapters/16-enough.html-82-    still runs. Every year on the anniversary he publishes a table of statistics, and he has done so
chapters/16-enough.html-83-    for eleven consecutive years, while the outcome was still unknown, with no investors to
chapters/16-enough.html-84-    impress.</p>
chapters/16-enough.html-85-
chapters/16-enough.html:86:    <p>At five years the surprise in the table was that there was none. The site <em>"makes roughly
chapters/16-enough.html-87-    the same amount of money (around $200K) every year"</em>, <em>"the number of active users stays
chapters/16-enough.html:88:    roughly the same"</em>, and in five years he had not received <em>"a single email from an
chapters/16-enough.html-89-    investor"</em>. He could have read that as a ceiling. He read it as the product: what a
chapters/16-enough.html-90-    bookmarking service sells is the belief that it will still be there, which requires
chapters/16-enough.html-91-    <em>"actually earning enough money to do that"</em>. Stability was not a symptom. It was the
chapters/16-enough.html-92-    offer.</p>
chapters/16-enough.html-93-
chapters/16-enough.html-94-    <p>Two years later he put the figures in. A revenue row joined the table in 2016 — deliberately,
--
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-80-    <p>Where he starts: Gymdesk <em>"had reached $12k in MRR in the middle of 2019"</em>, enough for
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-81-    him to leave his job and work on it full time — chapter five's threshold, met. He spent the rest
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-82-    of that year and the start of 2020 on the product — <em>"I contracted a very talented UX
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-83-    designer"</em> for the redesign, a task bought well, and it did what it was bought for — relaunched
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-84-    with a new interface, and then the gyms that were his whole market closed. <em>"We had significant churn from March to around
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-85-    August."</em> The year still ended up — <em>"having grown our MRR by 60% to around $19.5k"</em>
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html:86:    — but it ended with him doing every job, as he had for five years.</p>
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-87-
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-88-    <p>The first attempt to change that was not a hire. In 2021 he bought growth as a task, from
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html:89:    people whose business was selling it: <em>"After a few failed experiments engaging ‘growth’ and
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-90-    advertising agencies, I came to the conclusion that outside consultants and agencies were never
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-91-    going to understand our audience like I did."</em> His own reason is about the audience: outsiders <em>"were never going to understand our
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-92-    audience like I did."</em> Read as a purchase, that is the task form failing on its own terms —
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-93-    what he needed was not an output but someone inside the business, for as long as the business
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-94-    needed them.</p>
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-95-
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html:96:    <p>So: <em>"We made our first full time hire in June 2021"</em> — a person who <em>"used to
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-97-    write for us"</em>, with an SEO background, brought in-house as content marketer and editor to
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-98-    double down on the channel that already worked, organic search. The person had written for the business before the job existed. And the sentence he gives
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-99-    the moment is not about a budget: <em>"This was a big decision for me personally – it's different engaging freelancers for fixed
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-100-    term / cost work, than bringing on someone as a full time employee that you're now responsible
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-101-    to make sure they are paid every month. It was here that we started to make the transition from
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-102-    a one-man, owner run business to an actual company."</em></p>
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-103-
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-104-    <p>Later in 2021 came the second: <em>"I hired my first full time customer service person as
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-105-    well."</em> He had been buying that work before, as contractors: <em>"We have engaged customer
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-106-    service people through Upwork previously as contractors, and it just wasn't working out."</em>
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-107-    The work that had been eating his own day — support — was the second job he created, not the first, and it is the one he
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html:108:    passes judgment on: <em>"I still regret not doing this sooner."</em></p>
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-109-
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-110-    <p>Monthly revenue ended 2021 up <em>"by around x2.5"</em> on 2020. The business <em>"passed $1M
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html:111:    in ARR in mid 2022"</em>, and in 2022 kept hiring, <em>"ending the year with 8 employees and 4
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-112-    contractors."</em> The task-or-job decision was not made once, at the first hire, and then settled: at the end of
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-113-    2022 the business was paying eight people as jobs and buying four as tasks, side by side. He does
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-114-    not say which work went which way. He does say what the
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-115-    jobs cost him that the tasks had not: <em>"The more people I hire, the more work I need to do
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-116-    every day getting them “unstuck” when they encounter things outside of their comfort zone."</em>
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-117-    That sentence is where chapter eighteen begins; this one stops before it.</p>
--
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-137-    that buying them fresh each time throws away the part you were paying for.</p>
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-138-
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-139-    <p>The failure mode is the mirror of the one in chapter eighteen. There, documentation was the
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-140-    comfortable way not to hand work over; here, <em>keeping it a task</em> is the comfortable way
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-141-    not to take on a person. The platform contractor who has covered the same shift for a year is a
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-142-    job wearing a task's contract, and the arrangement has already given you most of a job's
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html:143:    dependence without any of its commitment. Galperin's support contractors ran that way for long enough that "I still regret not doing
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-144-    this sooner" is his whole verdict on it. The regret is not about the money. It is about the
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-145-    time.</p>
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-146-
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-147-    <p>Both forms are legitimate. The four contractors at Gymdesk at the end of 2022 are not a
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-148-    mistake in the post and are not one here. What is not legitimate is not knowing which one you
chapters/19-the-first-hire.html-149-    have — and this is where a business in the Netherlands stops choosing its own words, because on Dutch ground the state has a test of its own, in the box below.</p>
--
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-20-  },
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-21-  {
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-22-    "category": "backwards-causal",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-23-    "severity": "high",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-24-    "quote": "Galperin's agencies were tasks, bought correctly, and they failed for a reason the task form cannot fix: the work needed context that only accumulates in someone who stays.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-25-    "problem": "The source gives a different mechanism — agencies would not understand the audience — and does not show that the task form was correctly bought or that only an employee could have supplied the missing knowledge.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json:26:    "evidence": "Galperin, 12 May 2023: “After a few failed experiments engaging ‘growth’ and advertising agencies, I came to the conclusion that outside consultants and agencies were never going to understand our audience like I did. I decided to start building an in-house team, that would live and breath our product and vertical.” That is audience-knowledge and in-house context, not a demonstration that a specialist freelancer or a better-briefed agency was structurally incapable. “Bought correctly” is not in the post. This is the §8 failure: every quotation in the paragraph can be true while the arrow from “agencies failed” to “it had become a job” is the chapter’s.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-27-    "fix": "Attribute his conclusion as his: the agencies did not understand the audience, so he hired in-house. Treat “hours worth more the longer the same person does them” as a reasoned test for the reader, not as what this failure proved."
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-28-  },
