checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-60-  },
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-61-  {
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-62-    "category": "unsourced-claim",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-63-    "severity": "medium",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-64-    "quote": "The person was already doing the work for him, as a freelancer, before the job existed.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-65-    "problem": "“Used to write for us” does not establish that this person was a freelancer already doing the content-marketing/editor job, which is the entire case for “convert before you recruit”; the hire he says mattered more went the other way.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json:66:    "evidence": "Source: “I hired a person who used to write for us, and had a background in SEO, to be our in house content marketing / editor.” Claims.tsv registers only “used to write for us.” The freelancer contrast in the next paragraph is general, not about this person. The CS hire — “I still regret not doing this sooner” — came after Upwork contractors “just wasn’t working out” and a “new CS rep,” i.e. not a conversion. Monday item 4 still says “If they exist, the trial has already happened,” with no branch for a failed trial, so scored clause-by-clause against the chapter’s own test (§8) the case supports convert-when-the-trial-worked, not convert-first.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json:67:    "fix": "“The first employee was someone who used to write for him; he does not say for how long, or whether they were already doing the editor job.” Bound the principle: offer the job first where the same person has already done the work well. If the existing contractors failed, that is a completed trial against conversion — which is what his CS hire actually was."
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-68-  },
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-69-  {
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-70-    "category": "domain-error",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-71-    "severity": "medium",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-72-    "quote": "If the low month does not cover it, the hire is funded by revenue you have not yet had.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-73-    "problem": "A shortfall on chapter 13’s weekly cash figure is a cash fact; calling it “revenue you have not yet had” is the quantity confusion chapter 13 exists to stop.",
--
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-76-  },
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-77-  {
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-78-    "category": "unsourced-claim",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-79-    "severity": "medium",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-80-    "quote": "Eighteen months later a third of the people doing the work were still being bought as tasks, on purpose, next to the ones being paid as jobs.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-81-    "problem": "The 8/4 split is in the source; that it was a deliberate task-versus-job choice is not, and the next sentence admits he does not say which work went which way.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json:82:    "evidence": "Source: “In 2022 we continued to grow as a company, ending the year with 8 employees and 4 contractors.” Claims.tsv registers that split and nothing about intent. The chapter’s own following sentence: “He does not say which work went which way.” “On purpose” is what makes the split evidence that “both forms are legitimate” rather than leftover contractors, a hiring lag, or people who would not take employment.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-83-    "fix": "“Eighteen months later he still had four contractors next to eight employees. He does not say which work was which, or that the split was a task-or-job decision.” Keep the numbers; drop the intent."
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-84-  },
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-85-  {
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-86-    "category": "licence-to-wait",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-87-    "severity": "medium",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-88-    "quote": "The question is not whether this month's profit covers a salary. It is whether the revenue that repeats — chapter eleven's second test — covers it after acquisition and service, at the level you have now rather than the level you are hiring in order to reach.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-89-    "problem": "This is an evidence bar with no date by which the reader must stop waiting for the low month to cover the hire, and in a Part IV chapter whose own case is a regret about hiring too late.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json:90:    "evidence": "CONTEXT.md §3 standing constraint, rule 2: “Every evidence standard needs an expiry. A threshold with no date is an invitation to wait.” Rule 3 is partly met (Galperin’s “I still regret not doing this sooner”; dependence without commitment; the owed block’s liability). Monday item 3 restates the bar against the lowest of the last twelve cash months and still has no decide-by date. Combined with “cannot be cancelled in an afternoon,” the cautious reading is to wait another quarter. Monday items themselves are imperatives (rule 1 is clean).",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-91-    "fix": "Keep the funding test, then add the expiry the rest of the book requires: write the date by which the low month must cover the loaded cost, and what you will do on that date if it does not — hire a smaller role, keep buying a task, or accept the gap. Put the cost of waiting in the same breath: the work is already a job if the day does not run without that person."
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/grok.json-92-  }
