checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-119-    "problem": "“Used to write for us” does not establish that this person was a freelancer already doing the content-marketing/editor job, which is the entire case for “convert before you recruit”; the hire he says mattered more went the other way.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err:120:    "evidence": "Source: “I hired a person who used to write for us, and had a background in SEO, to be our in house content marketing / editor.” Claims.tsv registers only “used to write for us.” The freelancer contrast in the next paragraph is general, not about this person. The CS hire — “I still regret not doing this sooner” — came after Upwork contractors “just wasn’t working out” and a “new CS rep,” i.e. not a conversion. Monday item 4 still says “If they exist, the trial has already happened,” with no branch for a failed trial, so scored clause-by-clause against the chapter’s own test (§8) the case supports convert-when-the-trial-worked, not convert-first.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err:121:    "fix": "“The first employee was someone who used to write for him; he does not say for how long, or whether they were already doing the editor job.” Bound the principle: offer the job first where the same person has already done the work well. If the existing contractors failed, that is a completed trial against conversion — which is what his CS hire actually was."
