checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-143-    "problem": "This is an evidence bar with no date by which the reader must stop waiting for the low month to cover the hire, and in a Part IV chapter whose own case is a regret about hiring too late.",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err:144:    "evidence": "CONTEXT.md §3 standing constraint, rule 2: “Every evidence standard needs an expiry. A threshold with no date is an invitation to wait.” Rule 3 is partly met (Galperin’s “I still regret not doing this sooner”; dependence without commitment; the owed block’s liability). Monday item 3 restates the bar against the lowest of the last twelve cash months and still has no decide-by date. Combined with “cannot be cancelled in an afternoon,” the cautious reading is to wait another quarter. Monday items themselves are imperatives (rule 1 is clean).",
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-145-    "fix": "Keep the funding test, then add the expiry the rest of the book requires: write the date by which the low month must cover the loaded cost, and what you will do on that date if it does not — hire a smaller role, keep buying a task, or accept the gap. Put the cost of waiting in the same breath: the work is already a job if the day does not run without that person."
