checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-275-
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-276-    <p>Where he starts: Gymdesk <em>"had reached $12k in MRR in the middle of 2019"</em>, enough for
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-277-    him to leave his job and work on it full time — chapter five's threshold, met. He spent the rest
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-278-    of that year and the start of 2020 on the product, relaunched with a new interface, and then
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-279-    the gyms that were his whole market closed. <em>"We had significant churn from March to around
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-280-    August."</em> The year still ended up — <em>"having grown our MRR by 60% to around $19.5k"</em>
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err:281:    — but it ended with him doing every job, as he had for five years.</p>
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-282-
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-283-    <p>The first attempt to change that was not a hire. In 2021 he bought growth as a task, from
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err:284:    people whose business was selling it: <em>"After a few failed experiments engaging "growth" and
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-285-    advertising agencies, I came to the conclusion that outside consultants and agencies were never
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-286-    going to understand our audience like I did."</em> The task did not fail because the agencies
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-287-    were bad at tasks. It failed because what he needed was not an output. It was someone inside the
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-288-    business, for as long as the business needed them.</p>
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-289-
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err:290:    <p>So: <em>"We made our first full time hire in June 2021"</em> — a person who <em>"used to
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-291-    write for us"</em>, with an SEO background, brought in-house as content marketer and editor to
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-292-    double down on the channel that already worked, organic search. Two things in that sentence
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-293-    matter more than the role. The person was already doing the work for him, as a freelancer, before
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-294-    the job existed. And he did not experience the change as a budget line. By his own account:
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-295-    <em>"This was a big decision for me personally – it's different engaging freelancers for fixed
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-296-    term / cost work, than bringing on someone as a full time employee that you're now responsible
--
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-299-
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-300-    <p>Later in 2021 came the second: <em>"I hired my first full time customer service person as
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-301-    well."</em> He had been buying that work by the hour for years — <em>"We have engaged customer
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-302-    service people through Upwork previously as contractors, and it just wasn't working out."</em>
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-303-    Note the order. The work that had been eating his own day — support, the thing chapter seventeen
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-304-    would have ranked first — was the second job he created, not the first, and it is the one he
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err:305:    passes judgment on: <em>"I still regret not doing this sooner."</em></p>
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-306-
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-307-    <p>Monthly revenue ended 2021 up <em>"by around x2.5"</em> on 2020. The business <em>"passed $1M
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err:308:    in ARR in mid 2022"</em>, and in 2022 kept hiring, <em>"ending the year with 8 employees and 4
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-309-    contractors."</em> That last split is the quiet fact in the post. The
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-310-    task-or-job decision was not made once, at the first hire, and then settled. Eighteen months
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-311-    later a third of the people doing the work were still being bought as tasks, on purpose, next to
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-312-    the ones being paid as jobs. He does not say which work went which way. He does say what the
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-313-    jobs cost him that the tasks had not: <em>"The more people I hire, the more work I need to do
checks/reviews/19-the-first-hire/codex.err-314-    every day getting them “unstuck” when they encounter things outside of their comfort zone."</em>
--
