checks/sources.tsv:63:galperin-2023-covid	https://erangalperin.com/2023/05/12/building-a-fitness-tech-company-during-covid/	Eran Galperin — "Building A Fitness Tech Company During COVID", published 2023-05-12 (dateModified 2023-10-02), consulted 2026-08-06. Founder's own contemporaneous-retrospective account: the first full-time hire (content marketing/editor, June 2021) made after outside agencies failed, a second first-time hire for customer service after Upwork contractors were not working out, the framing of that decision as the shift from a one-man business to a company, and the end-2022 headcount split of 8 employees and 4 contractors. Founder's own account: weaker evidence class per §5, attributed as such in the prose. This is the "From business to company" predecessor the ch. 18 entry (§6i) named as linked in-text and left unfetched; the live page's title is "Building A Fitness Tech Company During COVID", not the linked anchor text.
checks/sources.tsv-64-# ch20 — Groove / Alex Turnbull, new company for the recurring cast. Case hunt in §6i.
checks/sources.tsv-65-groove-fire-2015	http://web.archive.org/web/20150316193110/https://www.groovehq.com/blog/how-to-fire-an-employee	Alex Turnbull / Groove — "Firing: How I Do the Hardest Thing as an Entrepreneur", published 2015-01-29, archived 2015-03-16, consulted 2026-08-06. Founder's own contemporaneous account, three years into Groove: the 2-4 week paid trial run before any permanent offer, direct feedback given often and logged (not saved for a end-of-trial review) during that trial, team and candidate input built into the decision, and the headcount-turnover figure from Groove's first three years. Founder's own account: weaker evidence class per §5, attributed as such in the prose.
checks/sources.tsv-66-# ch21 — Groove / Alex Turnbull, second use, different article and inference. Case hunt in §6i.
checks/sources.tsv-67-groove-meetings-2016	http://web.archive.org/web/20160305122806/https://www.groovehq.com/blog/how-to-optimize-remote-team-meetings	Alex Turnbull / Groove — "How We've Optimized Remote Team Meetings For Ultimate Efficiency", published 2016-02-18, archived 2016-03-05 (the capture closest to publication, chosen over a later 2018 capture because the later one silently redacted a named coach to "our coach" — the article text is otherwise identical), consulted 2026-08-06. Founder's own contemporaneous account of Groove's meeting rhythm: four earlier formats tried and abandoned or kept (no scheduled meetings, a weekly Monday call, an all-culture Friday call, an unstructured daily standup), the "9-person 45-minute" meetings that resulted, a video of 1-800-Got-Junk's own daily standup (nearly 20 people, under 7 minutes) that reset Turnbull's expectations, and the fixed, timeboxed, owned agenda structure adopted afterward. Founder's own account: weaker evidence class per §5, attributed as such in the prose. The 1-800-Got-Junk figures are Turnbull's secondhand relay of a video his business coach showed him, not sourced to 1-800-Got-Junk directly — flagged so the eventual draft attributes them two layers deep, not as this book's own verification of another company's meeting length.
checks/sources.tsv-68-# ch22 — ConvertKit / Nathan Barry, second use of the ch. 08 company, new source and new inference. Case hunt in §6i.
checks/sources.tsv-69-nb-metrics-2019	https://nathanbarry.com/metrics/	Nathan Barry — "Understanding ConvertKit's open metrics", published 2019-01-14, dateModified 2019-02-27, consulted 2026-08-06. Founder's own contemporaneous walkthrough of ConvertKit's public Baremetrics dashboard shortly after crossing $1m MRR: the five components of MRR movement, the MRR-versus-net-revenue distinction, user churn versus revenue versus net revenue churn, the quick ratio, ARPU against Buffer's, and the revenue concentration among higher-paying customers. Founder's own account: weaker evidence class per §5, attributed as such in the prose. Not an audited or independently verified figure set — the underlying numbers come from ConvertKit's own Baremetrics instance, which the post itself says is open to public view but which this book has not independently queried.
