checks/readers/09-what-to-charge/claude-cafe.md-1-**1. Gut reaction after first read**
checks/readers/09-what-to-charge/claude-cafe.md-2-
checks/readers/09-what-to-charge/claude-cafe.md:3:I nearly stopped at the Kickstarter. A rubber pen for a tablet, in America, in 2011 — what's that to do with a café in Darlington? But I kept going and by the middle I was nodding. The bit about hours not appearing in the customer's comparison is something I've watched chefs get wrong for twenty-five years and never had the words for.
checks/readers/09-what-to-charge/claude-cafe.md-4-
checks/readers/09-what-to-charge/claude-cafe.md-5-**2. What landed**
checks/readers/09-what-to-charge/claude-cafe.md-6-
checks/readers/09-what-to-charge/claude-cafe.md-7-"The buyer is comparing your offer with doing nothing, doing it themselves, hiring somebody else" — yes. Nobody buying a flat white is thinking about my rent. They're thinking about the Costa two doors down and the kettle at home. That's the whole game and he said it plainly.
checks/readers/09-what-to-charge/claude-cafe.md-8-
checks/readers/09-what-to-charge/claude-cafe.md-9-"Use the cost of replacing the work competently, not the comforting fiction that your own evenings are free." I've seen a dozen owners run themselves into the ground because they never counted their own hours. Blunt, and right.
--
checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md-7-"Your supplier is paid before your customer is" — that's the whole job in nine words. I've watched three restaurants go under on exactly that and none of the owners could have said it that plainly.
checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md-8-
checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md-9-"Stock bought on a quiet page is the way this trade dies while its shelves look full." I've seen the walk-in stacked with a fortnight of dairy on a wet Tuesday. That line I'd put on the wall.
checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md-10-
checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md-11-The floor in units. I don't want to know I need £2,100 a week; I want to know I need to sell 340 coffees and 90 lunches. That I can count on a Saturday and feel it in my stomach by two o'clock. And "keep the old number beside the new one" — that's the bit I wouldn't have thought of.
checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md-12-
checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md:13:"A screenshot of a transfer is not money until it clears" and "'I'll take it' in a chat... worth zero." Twenty-five years of people saying they'll book the function room.
checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md-14-
checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md-15-The three you turn away as menu, price and hours. "The seven o'clock full English you do not serve" — I've already had that argument with my sister. Nice to have it framed as a decision that buys something rather than me being awkward.
checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md-16-
checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md-17-**3. What didn't**
checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md-18-
checks/readers/13b-the-business-that-does-not-invoice/claude-cafe.md-19-"Move 1 The situation / Move 2 What actually happened / Move 3 The reasoning" — the scaffolding is clanky and I'm being asked to care about the book's method more than my café. Move 2 is a page and a half explaining why there's nothing in Move 2. "This is the one chapter in the book whose case is an absence, and the absence is the finding" — that's the sort of sentence that gets said on a stage with a headset mic.
--
checks/readers/06-who-is-this-for/claude-cafe.md-1-**1. Gut reaction after first read**
checks/readers/06-who-is-this-for/claude-cafe.md-2-
checks/readers/06-who-is-this-for/claude-cafe.md-3-I'll be honest, I nearly put it down at "$433 a month" and "MRR." It's about two lads and a bit of software that fights Google, and I'm opening a café in Darlington. But somewhere around "who you are going to disappoint on purpose" it stopped being about them and started being about me, and I finished it slightly cross that it was right.
checks/readers/06-who-is-this-for/claude-cafe.md-4-
checks/readers/06-who-is-this-for/claude-cafe.md-5-**2. What landed**
checks/readers/06-who-is-this-for/claude-cafe.md-6-
checks/readers/06-who-is-this-for/claude-cafe.md:7:"It is the rule you consult on ordinary days, when two things both look worth doing and there is time for one." Twenty-five years of that. Which supplier to ring back first, whose complaint gets a free coffee and whose gets a polite goodbye. Nobody ever called that positioning; it was just what the good managers did and the bad ones didn't.
checks/readers/06-who-is-this-for/claude-cafe.md-8-
checks/readers/06-who-is-this-for/claude-cafe.md-9-"A product shaped like its own support queue." I've run restaurants like that. Menus with forty items because every regular asked for one thing and the owner couldn't say no.
checks/readers/06-who-is-this-for/claude-cafe.md-10-
checks/readers/06-who-is-this-for/claude-cafe.md-11-"Name three kinds of customer who should not buy from you." That's the first business-book exercise I've ever seen that I could do on the back of a beer mat. Mine: people who want a full English at 7am, people who want to sit for three hours on one flat white with a laptop, and hen parties. There, done.
checks/readers/06-who-is-this-for/claude-cafe.md-12-
checks/readers/06-who-is-this-for/claude-cafe.md-13-"You can be compounding healthily and still read your own dashboard as proof that you are stuck." I don't have a dashboard, but I've watched owners panic in February and blow money on a refit when the numbers were actually fine.
--
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/drops/blocks.txt-47-[47] Pick your rung and name it. Deposit, non-refundable payment, or paid discovery — a small fixed-price piece of real work, invoiced first, which is the version of rung four that suits most consultancies. If you sell something nobody preorders — coffee, haircuts, plumbing — rung four is a standing order: the office across the road commits to twenty sandwiches every Tuesday for a month, paid for the first week now. Nobody puts a deposit on a flat white; somebody will on a month of them. Write down which, and why not one rung higher.
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/drops/blocks.txt-48-[48] Go to five people from chapter two's conversations and ask. Not "would you buy" — take the money today. Record each no word for word; the reason that repeats is the thing to fix.
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/drops/blocks.txt-49-[49] Write down what happens if it works too well. If forty say yes instead of five: who makes them, with what money, by when. Deciding while the number is hypothetical is the only time you will decide it calmly.
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/drops/blocks.txt-50-[50] Coolest Cooler — the demand test passed 265 times over
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/drops/blocks.txt-51-[51] In the summer of 2014 Ryan Grepper put a cooler on Kickstarter — with a built-in blender, a speaker and a USB charger. Goal: $50,000. The archived page records the close: "62,642 Backers · $13,285,226 pledged of $50,000 goal" — two hundred and sixty-five times the target. Just over fifty thousand took the main tier: "Pledge $185 or more · 50215 backers · COOLEST COOLER." And the page gave a date: "Estimated delivery: Feb 2015."
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/drops/blocks.txt-52-[52] On the first axis that is an unambiguous pass. Sixty-two thousand strangers made non-refundable payments, at $185, for an object that did not exist.
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/drops/blocks.txt:53:[53] Now the second axis. In February 2015 there were no coolers. According to Matthew Hughes in The Register, the company had "spruiked over 60,000 units costing $185 apiece" and, two years after the campaign, "asked backers for a further $97 in order for them to receive their product before the July 4 holidays of 2016." A settlement with the Oregon Department of Justice followed. It shut down in December 2019 — five years past its delivery date — "with 20,000 orders unfulfilled," owing those backers money that "cannot be discharged in bankruptcy."
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/drops/blocks.txt-54-[54] Why it collapsed is disputed and this chapter need not settle it; Grepper attributed the end to tariffs on Chinese imports, and there is no document here that would let anyone adjudicate that. The structural point survives whatever the cause. The pledge total was simultaneously the best demand evidence anyone in this book obtained and a contract to manufacture sixty thousand complex objects at a price fixed by someone who had never manufactured one. Both arrived in the same instant, from the same act, and only one of them felt like winning.
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/drops/blocks.txt-55-[55] The demand test worked exactly as advertised; the deliverability test was never cleared. That is why making, price and date sit on a separate axis rather than as a sixth rung.
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/drops/blocks.txt-56-[56] The counter-case this chapter does not have
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/drops/blocks.txt-57-[57] It would be someone who cleared both axes — independent buyers committed, making, price and date checked — delivered what they promised, and still had no business; or someone whose presale returned a clear no, who stopped and was wrong. Neither has been found in a checkable record, so the debt stands.
--
checks/readers/08-asking-for-money/claude-cafe.md-1-**1. Gut reaction after first read**
checks/readers/08-asking-for-money/claude-cafe.md-2-
checks/readers/08-asking-for-money/claude-cafe.md-3-I nearly did skip it, which I suppose is the point. It's about a man selling email software to bloggers, and I'm opening a café. But the bit about closed tabs being a no with no reason stuck — I've had that. People read the menu in the window and walk off, and I never find out why. That's the first time a business book has said something I'd said myself.
checks/readers/08-asking-for-money/claude-cafe.md-4-
checks/readers/08-asking-for-money/claude-cafe.md-5-**2. What landed**
checks/readers/08-asking-for-money/claude-cafe.md-6-
checks/readers/08-asking-for-money/claude-cafe.md:7:"Ask a successful founder what worked and you will often be told the name of an output of success, described as its cause." I've heard it for twenty-five years from owners: "It's the atmosphere." No it isn't, the atmosphere was built by the same people they later made redundant. That line felt like being let in on something.
checks/readers/08-asking-for-money/claude-cafe.md-8-
checks/readers/08-asking-for-money/claude-cafe.md-9-"Is anything frustrating you with MailChimp?" — one question, no pitch. I can do that. Standing outside my unit asking people where they get their coffee now and what annoys them about it. That's a Tuesday, not a strategy.
checks/readers/08-asking-for-money/claude-cafe.md-10-
checks/readers/08-asking-for-money/claude-cafe.md-11-The switching-cost bit: "the prospect placed all their possible objections on a single thing." I know that one. Customers don't leave because of five things, they leave because of one, and they'll tell you four polite ones first. And him doing the switch himself for free — that's what a good manager does, you just do the thing.
checks/readers/08-asking-for-money/claude-cafe.md-12-
checks/readers/08-asking-for-money/claude-cafe.md-13-"If you cannot fill five lines, that is the finding: you have been collecting silent nos and calling it market research." Ouch. Honest.
--
checks/readers/12-reading-your-own-business/claude-cafe.md-1-**1. Gut reaction after first read**
checks/readers/12-reading-your-own-business/claude-cafe.md-2-
checks/readers/12-reading-your-own-business/claude-cafe.md:3:I nearly put it down at "eenmanszaak." I'm opening a café in Lancashire, not Leiden, and half the chapter is Dutch law in Dutch. But the bit underneath the law — nobody is going to hand you the numbers that actually run the place, you have to build them yourself — that I recognised from twenty-five years of watching owners find out in March what went wrong in October. So: annoyed, then grudgingly nodding.
checks/readers/12-reading-your-own-business/claude-cafe.md-4-
checks/readers/12-reading-your-own-business/claude-cafe.md-5-**2. What landed**
checks/readers/12-reading-your-own-business/claude-cafe.md-6-
checks/readers/12-reading-your-own-business/claude-cafe.md-7-"Some months after the year has ended you receive a careful description of a period you can no longer do anything about." That's every set of year-end accounts I've ever seen an owner squint at. True, and it's said plainly.
checks/readers/12-reading-your-own-business/claude-cafe.md-8-
checks/readers/12-reading-your-own-business/claude-cafe.md-9-"Not the bank balance alone, which flatters — it contains VAT you are holding, payroll taxes, and customer deposits that were never yours." I've seen a manager spend the VAT. I've seen a *good* one do it. That line is worth the price of the chapter to somebody like me who's about to have one account with everything sloshing about in it.
--
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/claude-cafe.md-1-**1. Gut reaction after first read**
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/claude-cafe.md-2-
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/claude-cafe.md-3-I felt like I'd walked into a meeting for the wrong company. Kickstarter, iPhone clamps, sixty-two thousand strangers buying a cooler with a blender in it — I'm going to sell flat whites and bacon butties to people who work within five minutes of the door. But the bit about the man who took $13 million and ended up owing 20,000 people made my stomach turn, because I understood it instantly: he took the bookings and couldn't do the service.
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/claude-cafe.md-4-
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/claude-cafe.md-5-**2. What landed**
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/claude-cafe.md-6-
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/claude-cafe.md:7:"Everything people say about wanting your product is free to say. Evidence begins where saying it starts to cost them." That's twenty-five years of regulars saying "you should open your own place, love" in one line. None of them has put a deposit down.
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/claude-cafe.md-8-
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/claude-cafe.md-9-"Asking feels like a violation — you have spent a career in a job where money was somebody else's department." That's me exactly. I did the rotas, the staff, the front of house. Head office did the money. The author didn't say it to flatter me; he said it to tell me to get over it.
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/claude-cafe.md-10-
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/claude-cafe.md-11-The Coolest Cooler ending: "only one of them felt like winning." I've watched a chef take a 200-cover Christmas party because the number looked good and lose the regulars for a month. The bit about a hundred units on a kitchen table but sixty thousand can't be made "anywhere you have access to" — that's over-booking, and I know it in my bones.
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/claude-cafe.md-12-
checks/readers/03-selling-it-before-it-exists/claude-cafe.md-13-"However long you think the project will take to complete, you may do well to simply double that estimate." The builder quoted me six weeks. I'm writing twelve on the wall.
--
checks/readers/11-what-happens-after-the-sale/claude-cafe.md-1-**1. Gut reaction after first read**
checks/readers/11-what-happens-after-the-sale/claude-cafe.md-2-
checks/readers/11-what-happens-after-the-sale/claude-cafe.md:3:I'll be honest, when it opened on a software school in Denver I nearly put it down. I'm opening a café, not a coding bootcamp. But by the middle I'd stopped noticing that, because the idea underneath is one I've lived: the customers who go quiet are the ones you've lost, and nobody ever counts them. Twenty-five years of managing and I never once saw a manager track that.
checks/readers/11-what-happens-after-the-sale/claude-cafe.md-4-
checks/readers/11-what-happens-after-the-sale/claude-cafe.md-5-**2. What landed**
checks/readers/11-what-happens-after-the-sale/claude-cafe.md-6-
checks/readers/11-what-happens-after-the-sale/claude-cafe.md-7-"They do not complain. They go quiet, and quiet reads like contentment in every system you are likely to have built." That's exactly it. A table that never comes back doesn't send a letter. I've watched owners look at a full Saturday and think all's well while the Tuesday regulars drifted off.
checks/readers/11-what-happens-after-the-sale/claude-cafe.md-8-
checks/readers/11-what-happens-after-the-sale/claude-cafe.md-9-"Count from the population that paid, not the population that finished. Everything else is arithmetic performed on survivors." Short, plain, and I'll remember it. For me it's: everyone who walked in, not everyone who ordered a second coffee.

